  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM EDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nThe pattern turns wetter this weekend. More amplified flow aloft \nappears likely, with a trough descending over the Ohio Valley and \nthe upper level ridge centered over the Desert Southwest. A weak sfc \nlow should accompany the upper level shortwave trough, moving over \nthe Lower OH Valley on Saturday. This wave then occludes and weakens \nbefore shifting off to the southeast heading into early next week. \n\nA round of widespread rainfall looks likely on Saturday, with rain \npossibly moderate to heavy at times. However, a significant \nrainfall/flooding event is not expected. Instability will be \nsomewhat muted by the upper trough and widespread cloud cover, which \nshould also help limit the severe weather potential. LREF mean PW \nvalues are around 1.7 inches, which is elevated but not \nsignificantly anomalous (though notable model spread lingers this \nweekend into early next week). \n\nLighter rainfall appears likely on Sunday with the stacked low \noverhead. Highs over the weekend should range from the upper 70s to \nmid 80s. At least diurnally-driven showers and thunderstorms linger \nthrough early next week, and the majority of LREF members show at \nleast light rain each day. However, forecast confidence in the \ndetails is fairly low by Monday and Tuesday.",
